Z||000000||GarethWright||11-29-2001||05:45 PM||||Hello all, <BR>I am working on a 649 cc Motorcycle engine, and I need to know what sort of compression I should be getting, its a 4 stroke OHV horizontally opposed twin, and heres the numbers I have for it:<P>Displacement:      649 CC<BR>Cylinder Bore:     78mm/3.07in<BR>Piston Stroke:     68mm/2.68in<BR>Compression Ratio: 8.5<P>Right now I am getting about 45 PSI in the left cylinder and about 30 PSI in the right.  I know the engine needs some work, as it was built in 1971 and has 4km on it.<BR>For those of you interested, its a Ural motorcycle <A HREF="http://www.ural.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ural.com</A> <BR>thanks in advance<BR>Gareth<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GarethsSig2.GIF"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||11-29-2001||07:08 PM||||Well, I don't have any specs for you, but I can tell you it needs to be torn apart and inspected.<P>Almost all four stroke motors won't run, or will barely run, with less than 80 or 90 PSI.<P>Also, motors have too much variance to use a calculation to estimate what the cylinder PSI will be.  There are too many variants like cam timing, ring sealing, valve sealing, etc. that vary between every engine.  Also, with a used motor, none of this could be calculated, as every motor will be different.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
